### Production Context

When writing high-throughput domain logic in C#, heavy use of immutable value types or custom mathematical structs can quickly pollute the Gen0 heap if not handled carefully. Historically, writing `vector = vector + delta;` meant the runtime allocated a temporary instance of the struct, even when working entirely on the stack.

C# 14 and .NET 10 introduce direct support for overloading custom compound assignment operators (`+=`, `-=`) using ref parameters. This lets us update large value types in-place without triggering defensive copying or hidden temporary allocations.

### Before

```csharp
public readonly struct Vector(double x, double y)
{
    public double X { get; } = x;
    public double Y { get; } = y;

    public static Vector operator +(Vector a, Vector b)
        => new(a.X + b.X, a.Y + b.Y);
}

// Usage triggers an intermediate struct instantiation
Vector v = new(1.0, 2.0);
v = v + new(3.0, 4.0);
```

### After

```csharp
public struct Vector(double x, double y)
{
    public double X = x;
    public double Y = y;

    public static void operator +=(ref Vector a, Vector b)
    {
        a.X += b.X;
        a.Y += b.Y;
    }
}

// Usage mutates the stack frame directly with zero overhead
Vector v = new(1.0, 2.0);
v += new(3.0, 4.0);
```

### Why it matters

- **Zero Allocations:** Eliminates intermediate struct instantiation during tight mathematical or spatial calculations.
- **Direct Stack Mutation:** Operates on references directly, keeping CPU cache lines hot.
- **Cleaner Syntax:** Maintains readable arithmetic syntax while delivering low-level span-like performance.

### Watch out

Be mindful when applying `ref` to struct operators if your types are shared across multiple threads or expected to behave immutably. Always verify your hot paths using BenchmarkDotNet to confirm the allocation drop.

